# Confronting Cognitive Degradation

## Observation of a Video Call
I had a video call earlier today. During this call, we were discussing different personal situations that have been affecting myself and the individual I had the awesome opportunity to converse with. 

We were talking about very important life matters. Both of us were unsure if the actions or routes we chose to take—or are currently taking—are the smartest and only options available to us. As the conversation went on, things weren't becoming one-sided or intense, but they were getting incredibly serious. One of us started pacing back-and-forth. 

## The Breakdown of Communication
As we continued, certain cognitive and physical functions started to slip. Originally, we were able to respond within a 1-to-3 second window. This gave us enough time to properly comprehend, process, and respond accordingly. We were able to use rich vocabulary and structure our sentences carefully, maintaining the kind of situational awareness, posture, and appropriate dialect you would need to uphold in a professional environment.

Up until that point, before either of us became physically exasperated or distracted, we were having a positive, beneficial, and constructive conversation. 

However, as other things gradually grabbed our attention—like making sure we weren't walking into someone, watching our tone, and ensuring our decibel levels weren't too loud or annoying to people around us—our cognitive function started to disappear. 

## Physical & Cognitive Symptoms
Personally, I had to start processing my responses heavily. I wasn't able to respond immediately anymore. Instead of keeping my attention on my work or things that needed to be done, I started pacing as well. 

The other individual wasn't aware of the notes I was mentally keeping, but around this time, they experienced a physical reaction. They could feel, and I could see on the screen, that their eyes started to flutter and fluctuate. I immediately tried to help by telling them to calm down, gain control, and by giving them grounding instructions. The individual started to panic and became quite distressed, worrying that the reaction might be genetic or caused by past physical injuries.

## Personal Reflection on Motor Control
When I notice this cognitive wandering happening to myself, I realize that simply trying to force your eyes to sit still isn't enough. You can control the flutter if you concentrate intensely in the moment. However, the exact second you let go, or the moment you are distracted by another task, your eyes will start to flick or wander to things around you. 

The more I practice focusing, the more I can feel a physical connection on the right side of my body that I cannot easily feel on my left. To actually maintain a stable, healthy connection and control what I don't naturally feel, consistent practice is key. Sometimes, it takes hours of concentration every single day.
